CONVOLVE
++++++++

SUMMARY
-------
Compute the convolution of a pulse shape with all other time series in memory.

SYNTAX
------
::

  CONVOLVE  {FILENAME} {PULSE n} {BOX v} {TRI v} {GAUSS v} {TRAP v1 v2}

INPUT
-----

   :PULSE n: The PULSE is filenumber n

   :FILENAME: Convolve with data in FILENAME

   :BOX v: Convolve with a box of width v seconds

   :TRI v: Convolve with a triangle of half width v seconds

   :GAUSS v: Convolve with a gaussian with standard deviation v seconds

   :TRAP v1 v2: Convolve with a trapezoid of rise v1 and width v2

   :CENTERED ON|OFF: Place zero time on center [ON] or beginning [OFF] of the pulse

DEFAULT VALUES
--------------
::

  CONVOLVE PULSE 1 CENTERED OFF

DESCRIPTION
-----------
  In seismology, convolution is used in two ways:

    1) convolving a pulse shape with a time-series, and
    2) convolving (or, more typically, deconvolving) a response with a time series.

  The equation for convolution takes a simple form in both the time
  and frequency domains; (1) is more easily described in the time
  domain, while (2) in the frequency domain.  The CONVOLVE function
  here is oriented towards (1).  Function TRANSFER_ is more
  appropriate for (2).

  The pulse file is convolved with all other the time-series files in
  memory using the discretized version of the following equation::

      conv(tau) = int ( f(t) g(tau - t) ) dt,

  where f is the pulse file, g any from among the other signals in
  memory, and int means integration (summation) over all times t,
  or integration. All signals in memory must have the same DELTA. The
  number of points in f, the pulse shape, cannot be greater than the
  number of points in any other file in memory. If npts(g) is the number
  of points in g and npts(f) the number in f, the number in conv is
  npts(g) + npts(f) - 1. The start time for each output conv will be the
  same as that for the input g.  Centering on the pulse shapes eliminates
  time shifts introduced by convolution.  If CENTERED is OFF, the first
  data point of the pulses is defined as zero.  CENTERED ON should only be used
  for time-symmetric pulses.

PREDEFINED FUNCTIONS
--------------------
  The option to convolve with BOX, TRI, GAUSS, or TRAP automatically
  computes these function with the correct time samplings.  If one
  uses one of these options, there is no pulse-shape file read in; the
  predefined function takes the place of f(t) in the above equation.
  All Functions are normalized such that they integrate to 1.0 and
  should not add amplitude, or "moment" in the language of sources, to
  the final, convolved signal.  All functions are time-symmetric.

RELATION TO CORRELATE
---------------------
  Note the similarity in the convolve equation and the one in CORRELATE_.
  Many convolve routines, including the original one in SAC, mistakenly
  use this similarity in deriving the procedure for convolution by simply
  perturbing the procedure used in CORRELATE_. The time series from a
  convolution (conv in the equation above) is typically a replacement time
  series for the time series g; for example, adding a pulse shapee to a
  synthetic seismogram.  The relative times for f and g are not used in
  CORRELATE_, but in convolution they may play a role.

  For example, g might be a synthetic time series of spikes with
  time offsets, amplitudes, and polarities representing phase arrivals
  The synthetic may have Gibbs phenomena accompanying each arrival.
  A way to improve the appearance is to convolve g with a time-symmetric
  triangle function, e.g. ::

     SAC> fg triangle npts 8 delta 0.02 begin -0.08

  If one did not take into account the start time of f, the arrivals in conv
  would be offset by 0.08 seconds.  A script showing runs with f given by
  both that triangle pulse and also a pulse shape starting at time zero
  is included in ${SACHOME}/doc/examples/convolve.

